# Runtime dependencies of the MCP / Agent Bridge (`mcp_bridge/`) — a SEPARATE, OPTIONAL component.
# Compiled (NO hashes, cross-platform) to requirements-mcp.txt:
#   pip-compile requirements-mcp.in -o requirements-mcp.txt
#
# WHAT THIS IS NOT. It is not part of the shipped Windows app. requirements.in → requirements-win.txt
# (hashed, win_amd64) is what klarpdf-setup-x64.exe bundles, and it does not contain a line of this;
# the installer's size, its offline lock and its clean-machine install test are all untouched
# (PLAN.md §Dependencies & packaging). It is also not requirements-dev.txt, which carries `mcp`
# only so CI can run tests/test_mcp_*.py. This file is what a *bridge user* installs.
#
# NO HASHES, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE — getting it wrong makes the bridge accidentally Windows-only.
# The server is platform-independent even though the app is not: model/ has no platform-specific
# code, PyMuPDF ships manylinux/macOS/Windows wheels, and mcp's only Windows dependency (pywin32) is
# platform-marked. A `--generate-hashes` lock is per-platform by construction and `--require-hashes`
# then fails on every other OS by design (CLAUDE.md §Gotchas), which would defeat the entire
# audience — developers running agentic clients, most of them not on Windows.
#
# PyMuPDF IS THE PDF ENGINE BOTH SURFACES SHARE, so it is pinned to the app's exact version — not
# to a floor (M115). A floor cannot hold two locks together: `pip-compile` resolves `>=` to whatever
# was newest on the day it ran, so the same `>=1.25.5` that gave the app 1.27.2.3 gave this file
# 1.28.2 the next time it was compiled, and the bridge and the GUI spent three months writing PDFs
# with different MuPDF builds. This line must equal the PyMuPDF pin in `requirements-win.txt`;
# `tests/test_dependency_parity.py` fails if it does not. Bump both together, never one.
#
# pypdf is absent on purpose — the bridge never uses PyPdfEngine.
PyMuPDF==1.27.2.3
mcp>=2,<3
